description:
MIP-3 sections 4, 6 and 7. Builds on a_2XJ1ffNcD-a_2XJ1gDR4W. This is where the daemon becomes a terminal host.

Output:
- a reader thread per pane sends each chunk to the attached bridge first, then writes it into the headless terminal; nothing on that path waits for another pane, a request, or a replay;
- credit flow control: the bridge acknowledges bytes written to the surface; at most a window (256 KB to start) unacknowledged per pane; a pane that falls behind is caught up with the screen only, not history;
- attach: register the bridge at the recorded offset under the pane's lock, then send the composed replay from a_2XJ1ffNcD; format long histories from a copy outside the lock;
- one bridge per pane; a second attach must ask for takeover or is refused, and the displaced bridge is told why;
- kitty graphics: storage on with a small limit, file/temp-file/shm media refused.

Input and replies:
- the input connection carries structured key, mouse, focus and paste events; each pane has one writer thread and queue for keystrokes, pastes, `pane send` text and query answers, in order; the encoder reads a copy of the pane's modes updated after each chunk; a full queue drops query answers rather than blocking the reader;
- mouse and wheel: the daemon decides from the pane's modes between a report, alternate-scroll arrows, and nothing (Ghostty's `Surface.zig:3570-3625`);
- paste is fenced for bracketed paste; a newline-bearing paste to a pane without it is reported unsafe instead of written;
- the daemon answers DA1/DA2, CPR, XTVERSION, size reports in cells and pixels, kitty keyboard, XTGETTCAP, OSC 4/10/11/12 from the palette the app sends (persisted for when no app is connected), and emits mode 2031 notifications on appearance change;
- effects (title, OSC 7, bell, OSC 9/777, OSC 9;4, OSC 52 writes) become events.

The latency tier's flood and remote-flood cases (MIP-3 section 13) are the acceptance test for the flow control; they can land here or with the vertical slice.
